<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net" modified="2026-06-11T00:00:00.000Z" agent="Trae"><diagram id="pipeline" name="Pipeline"><mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="2600" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2200" pageHeight="3200" math="0" shadow="0"><root><mxCell id="0" /><mxCell id="1" parent="0" /><mxCell id="header" value="SciAtlas Idea Generation Pipeline&#10;Run dir: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/chemstry_question/09-&#10;生命系统繁殖/full&#10;Topic: Research question: What drives reproduction in&#10;living systems?&#10;Context: Reproduction is the manner in which organisms&#10;ensure their survival. Individuals with genetic traits&#10;that give them advantages will reproduce more often&#10;through natural selection.&#10;Status: ok&#10;Failed step: N/A" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="40" y="40" width="435" height="222" /></mxCell><mxCell id="params" value="Input Parameters&#10;topic: Research question: What drives reproduction in&#10;living systems?&#10;Context: Reproduction is the manner in which organisms&#10;ensure their survival. Individuals with genetic traits&#10;that give them advantages will reproduce more often&#10;through natural selection.&#10;domain: Chemistry&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;&#10;Effective Pipeline Config&#10;workflow_mode: full&#10;anchor_top_k: 5&#10;keyword_min_papers: 2&#10;k_step1: 4&#10;seed_num_queries: 8&#10;seed_per_query: 3&#10;seed_request_multiplier: 6&#10;seed_request_floor: 12&#10;seed_recent_years: 7&#10;seed_min_citations: 1000&#10;graph_budget_ratio: 0.35&#10;graph_budget_min: 12&#10;graph_budget_max: 30&#10;graph_max_predecessors_per_paper: 3&#10;graph_min_forward_papers: 3&#10;graph_candidate_fetch_limit: 40&#10;num_candidate_step2: 3&#10;num_expansion_step2: 1&#10;innoeval_root: /REDACTED/tiaozhanbei/SciAtlas/referen&#10;ces/search/src/innoeval_search&#10;grobid_base_url: http://127.0.0.1:8070&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_same_field: 2&#10;inspiration_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;max_novelty_feedback_rounds: 2&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;runs_dir: runs&#10;&#10;Effective SciAtlas Config&#10;base_url: http://sciatlas.openkg.cn&#10;api_key: ***REDACTED***&#10;timeout: 900&#10;max_retries: 4&#10;retry_backoff: 5.0&#10;use_cache: True&#10;cache_dir: runs/cache&#10;official_cli_root: .&#10;use_local_kg: False&#10;local_search_root: references/search" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="40" width="442" height="942" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step1" value="Step 1 Anchor Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;seed_paper_count: 4" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="340" width="220" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step2" value="Step 2 Research Graph Construction&#10;status: completed&#10;graph_paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="500" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step1_step_step2" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="step_step2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step2_graph_title" value="Step 2 Annotated Research Graph&#10;paper_count: 4&#10;edge_count: 0&#10;phase_count: 1&#10;directions: anchor=4, backward=0,&#10;forward=0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=13;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="500" width="267" height="132" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_title" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step2_graph_title"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed" value="Seed Paper 1/4&#10;Genetic variance in reproductive&#10;timing contributes to trait&#10;evolvability&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4416670776&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="672" width="260" height="164" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_0" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_1" value="Seed Paper 2/4&#10;Sex-specific selection patterns in&#10;a dioecious insect-pollinated&#10;plant&#10;year: 2023&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4366817449&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="640" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_1" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_2" value="Seed Paper 3/4&#10;Assortative mating can help&#10;adaptation of flowering time to a&#10;changing climate: Insights from a&#10;polygenic model&#10;year: 2021&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W3141331266&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="920" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_2" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_2"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_seed_3" value="Seed Paper 4/4&#10;Asymmetric life-history trade-offs&#10;shape sex-biased longevity&#10;patterns&#10;year: 2025&#10;paper_id:&#10;https://openalex.org/W4412678672&#10;direction: anchor&#10;evidence_score: 1.0" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#cfe2f3;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="1200" y="672" width="260" height="180" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step2_graph_seed_3" value="seed" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#3d85c6;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step2_graph_title" target="graph_seed_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 9265&#10;trend_chars: 10006" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="952" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: What drives reproduction&#10;in living systems? Context: Reproduction is the manner in&#10;which organisms ensure their survival. Individuals with&#10;genetic traits that give them advantages will reproduce more&#10;often through natural selection. seed_paper: Genetic variance&#10;in reproductive timing contributes to trait evolvability #&#10;CITATION SUBGRAPH (3 refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1]&#10;Sex-specific selection patterns in a dioecious insect-&#10;pollinated plant (2023, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE]&#10;layer_depth = 1 cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 24&#10;section_weight = 0.0 delta_year = unknown is_influential =&#10;False low_confidence = True cited_by_subgraph = 0&#10;[PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[] delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none&#10;[IDEA] Problem: Competition for mate acquisition is the&#10;hallmark of any sexual organism. In insect-pollinated plants,&#10;competition to attract pollinators is expected to result in&#10;pollinator-mediated selection on attractive floral traits.&#10;This could overlap with sexual selection if the number of&#10;mating partners incre Existing Methods: Unavailable from local&#10;metadata. Motivation: Competition for mate acquisition is the&#10;hallmark of any sexual organism. In insect-pollinated plants,&#10;competition to attract pollinators is expected to result in&#10;pollinator-mediated selection on attractive floral traits.&#10;This could overlap with sexual selection if the number of&#10;mating partners incre Proposed Method: Competition for mate&#10;acquisition is the hallmark of any sexual organism. In insect-&#10;pollinated plants, competition to attract pollinators is&#10;expected to result in pollinator-mediated selection on&#10;attractive floral traits. This could overlap with sexual&#10;selection if the number of mating partners incre Experiment&#10;Plan: Unavailable from local metadata. [ABSTRACT] Competition&#10;for mate acquisition is the hallmark of any sexual organism.&#10;In insect-pollinated plants, competition to attract&#10;pollinators is expected to result in pollinator-mediated&#10;selection on attractive floral traits. This could overlap with&#10;sexual selection if the number of mating partners increases&#10;with pollinator attraction, resulting in an improved&#10;reproductive success. In this study, we measured a set of&#10;floral traits and estimated individual fitness in male and&#10;female Silene dioica in an experimental population. Results&#10;align with the predictions of Bateman's principles, in the&#10;absence of pollen limitation. In females, natural selection&#10;acted on traits that are typically linked with fertility&#10;(number of flowers and number of gametes), and selection&#10;strength was similar in open- and hand-pollinated females,&#10;suggesting a limited role of pollinator-mediated selection. In&#10;males, flowering duration and corolla width were positively&#10;associated with both reproductive success and number of mates,&#10;suggesting that sexual selection has played a role in the&#10;evolution of these traits. The use of Bateman's metrics&#10;further confirmed stronger sexual selection in males than in&#10;females. Taken together, our results shed light on the&#10;occurrence of sex-specific patterns of selection in an insect-&#10;pollinated plant population. ## [2] Assortative mating can&#10;help adaptation of...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: Paper 0 to Paper 1: Paper 0 is best read as the 2021&#10;modeling study, *Assortative mating can help adaptation of&#10;flowering time to a changing climate: Insights from a&#10;polygenic model*. It treats reproductive timing as a polygenic&#10;trait shaped by nonrandom mating. Its central advance is to&#10;show that assortative mating can have antagonistic effects on&#10;genetic variance: it creates positive associations among&#10;similar allelic effects, which can inflate genetic variance,&#10;but it can also reduce polymorphism, which depresses variance.&#10;Under environmental change, however, assortative mating can&#10;increase standing genetic variance, reduce the lag between the&#10;population and the moving optimum, and provide a fitness&#10;advantage relative to random mating. This connects directly to&#10;the seed idea that genetic variance in reproductive timing&#10;contributes to trait evolvability. Paper 1, the 2023 study&#10;*Sex-specific selection patterns in a dioecious insect-&#10;pollinated plant*, shifts the focus from abstract mating-&#10;system effects on genetic variance to the concrete phenotypic&#10;and sex-specific selection acting on reproductive traits. In&#10;*Silene dioica*, female fitness is more strongly associated&#10;with fertility-linked traits such as flower number and gamete&#10;production, while male fitness is shaped by flowering&#10;duration, corolla width, reproductive success, and number of&#10;mates. The transition therefore moves from “how mating&#10;patterns preserve or reshape genetic variation in reproductive&#10;timing” to “how that variation is translated into sex-specific&#10;reproductive success through natural selection, pollinator-&#10;mediated selection, and sexual selection.” Paper 1 to Paper 2:&#10;Paper 1 emphasizes mate acquisition, floral trait selection,&#10;and sex-specific reproductive success. Paper 2, the 2025 study&#10;*Asymmetric life-history trade-offs shape sex-biased longevity&#10;patterns*, broadens the evolutionary frame from reproductive&#10;trait expression to the allocation trade-off between&#10;reproduction and survival. It models monogamous individuals&#10;evolving under sex-specific reproductive constraints: males&#10;trade survival against mating opportunities, while females&#10;trade survival against offspring production. The key result is&#10;that either sex can evolve greater longevity, but male-biased&#10;longevity evolves under a wider range of conditions because&#10;female longevity is more tightly constrained by offsprin..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="952" width="560" height="2112" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3164" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Evolutionary ecology and quantitative life-&#10;history genetics of reproduction, focusing on how genetic&#10;variation, reproductive timing, mate acquisition, sex-specific&#10;selection, and survival trade-offs drive reproductive success&#10;in changing environments.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Existing approaches isolate major drivers of&#10;reproduction, such as genetic variance in reproductive timing,&#10;mate acquisition or sexual selection, and reproduction-&#10;survival trade-offs, so the field lacks a predictive, sex-&#10;aware, environmentally contingent framework that explains how&#10;genetic architecture, mating ecology, and life-history&#10;allocation jointly determine when reproductive strategies&#10;increase fitness and evolvability versus when they create&#10;constraints.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- reproductive timing or flowering time&#10;- genetic variance and polygenic architecture&#10;- assortative mating or nonrandom mating&#10;- mate acquisition and mating success&#10;- sexual selection and Bateman-style fitness gradients&#10;- fertility traits such as flower number, gamete production,&#10;and offspring production&#10;- attractive or competitive reproductive traits such as&#10;corolla width and flowering duration&#10;- individual reproductive success or fitness&#10;- sex-specific selection&#10;- reproduction-survival trade-offs&#10;- longevity and ageing&#10;- ecological context such as climate change, pollinators, and&#10;density-dependent mortality&#10;- mating system structure such as monogamy, dioecy, and&#10;assortative mating&#10;- phenological mismatch between reproductive timing, mates,&#10;pollinators, and environment&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- Genetic variance in reproductive timing affects the ability&#10;of populations to track environmental optima and maintain&#10;evolvability.&#10;- Assortative mating can increase genetic variance and reduce&#10;adaptive lag under directional environmental change, but can&#10;also reduce polymorphism.&#10;- Sex-specific selection maps different traits to fitness in&#10;males and females, with male success often tied to mate&#10;attraction or mating opportunities and female success often&#10;tied to fertility-linked traits.&#10;- Reproductive effort competes with survival, shaping sex-&#10;biased longevity under different mating systems and trade-off&#10;strengths.&#10;- Ecological agents such as pollinators, mate availability,&#10;and mortality regimes modulate the strength and direction of&#10;selection on reproductive traits.&#10;- Temporal overlap and phenological matching condition mating&#10;opportunities and reproductive success.&#10;- Multivariate trait correlations can constrain or facilitate&#10;the evolution of integrated reproductive strategies.&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- Strong context dependence of mating-system and selection&#10;outcomes across environments.&#10;- Simplified mating systems and ecological assumptions in&#10;current models.&#10;- Incomplete integration among genetic variance, sex-specific&#10;selection, and demographic life-history feedbacks.&#10;- Difficulty measuring mating success, fertility, survival,&#10;and environmental conditions simultaneously across&#10;generations.&#10;- Sex-specific reproductive mechanisms are described with&#10;separate frameworks rather than a unified theory.&#10;- Multivariate reproductive trait evolution remains&#10;underdeveloped.&#10;- Phenological mismatch among reproductive timing, mates,&#10;pollinators, and environmental change is not fully resolved.&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- Assortative mating can both inflate genetic variance and&#10;depress polymorphism.&#10;- Sexual selection for mate attraction can conflict with&#10;survival or fertility optimization.&#10;- Male reproductive success through mating opportunities may&#10;be less constrained than female success through offspring&#10;production, producing asymmetric longevity evolution.&#10;- Short-term reproductive advantage may conflict with long-&#10;term survival and adaptive resilience.&#10;- Environmental change can make nonrandom mating beneficial,&#10;while stabilizing conditions can weaken or reverse those&#10;benefits.&#10;- Extrinsic mortality can obscure evolved sex-specific&#10;longevity differences.&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- Integrate the genetic architecture of reproductive timing&#10;with mate acquisition and fertility selection.&#10;- Represent sex-specific fitness pathways and constraints&#10;explicitly.&#10;- Include reproduction-survival allocation and longevity&#10;outcomes.&#10;- Accommodate changing environments, phenological mismatch,&#10;and density-dependent mortality.&#10;- Generalize across mating systems or clearly specify boundary&#10;conditions.&#10;- Handle multivariate correlated reproductive traits rather&#10;than single-trait axes.&#10;- Generate testable predictions linking variance maintenance,&#10;selection, and life-history outcomes.&#10;- Bridge theoretical predictions with measurable empirical&#10;variables across generations.&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- coupled quantitative-genetic and demographic models&#10;- sex-specific life-history allocation rules&#10;- assortment or temporal-overlap mating kernels&#10;- multivariate selection-gradient frameworks&#10;- eco-evolutionary feedback mechanisms&#10;- state-dependent resource-allocation mechanisms&#10;- phenological matching and mismatch response rules&#10;- variance-maintenance mechanisms under fluctuating selection&#10;- adaptive trade-off optimization under mating-system&#10;constraints&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- life-history theory on reproduction-survival trade-offs&#10;- quantitative genetics of polygenic reproductive timing&#10;- sexual selection and Bateman gradient studies&#10;- plant pollination ecology and phenological mismatch&#10;- mating-system evolution across monogamous, polygamous,&#10;dioecious, and hermaphroditic systems&#10;- demographic models with density-dependent mortality&#10;- multivariate evolution and genetic correlation theory&#10;- long-term multigenerational field studies of fitness,&#10;mating, and survival&#10;- comparative sex-specific ageing and longevity research" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3164" width="560" height="2508" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: completed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="5772" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed to remain grounded&#10;in evolutionary ecology, quantitative genetics, sexual&#10;selection, and life-history theory. Cross-domain retrieval is&#10;justified because the RSS highlights unresolved integration&#10;across genetic architecture, sex-specific selection, mating&#10;ecology, survival trade-offs, and environmental mismatch,&#10;suggesting that mechanism transfer from external modeling&#10;frameworks may help build a unified predictive structure." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="5772" width="560" height="294" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="6166" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: The Influence of Sexual Selection and Sexual Conflict&#10;on the Evolution of Post-Insemination Dynamics&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Sexual reproduction is a fundamental process that&#10;structures populations and modulates interactions between&#10;species. The reproductive process is shaped by selection&#10;acting on the variance in mating success. Additionally,&#10;conflict between the sexes over the mechanisms by which...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Sexual selection studies: A NESCent catalyst meeting&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: A catalyst meeting on sexual selection studies was&#10;held in July 2013 at the facilities of the National&#10;Evolutionary Synthesis Center (NESCent) in Durham, NC. This&#10;article by a subcommittee of the participants foregrounds some&#10;of the topics discussed at the meeting. Topics ment...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: economics&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: An optimisation principle for sex allocation in a&#10;temporally varying environment&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: economics&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Resource Elasticity of Offspring Survival and the&#10;Optimal Evolution of Sex Ratios&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: The fitness of any organisms includes the survival&#10;and reproductive rate of adults and the survival of their&#10;offspring. Environmental selection pressures might not affect&#10;these two aspects of an organism equally. Assuming that an&#10;organism first allocates its limited resources...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 5&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Agent-Based Modelling as a Tool to Advance Evolutionary&#10;Population Theory&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 6&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: A demogenetic individual based model for the evolution&#10;of traits and genome architecture under sexual selection&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Abstract Sexual selection has long been known to&#10;favor the evolution of mating behaviors such as mate&#10;preference and competitiveness, and to affect their genetic&#10;architecture, for instance by favoring genetic correlation&#10;between some traits. Reciprocally, genetic architecture..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fde9d9;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="6166" width="620" height="1230" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step6_candidates_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step6" target="step6_candidates_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step7" value="Step 7 Analogy Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;inspiration_count: 6&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="7496" width="220" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step6_step_step7" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step6" target="step_step7"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step7_analogy_panel" value="Step 7 Analogy Results&#10;count: 6&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: The Influence of Sexual Selection and Sexual Conflict&#10;on the Evolution of Post-Insemination Dynamics&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Lifecycle-explicit decomposition of&#10;selection into sex-specific reproductive stages',&#10;'Quantitative accounting of sexual conflict and genetic load&#10;as constraints on adaptation', 'Stage-specific linking of&#10;mating success, gametic/post-insemination dynamics, and&#10;viability selection', 'Separating true sexually antagonistic&#10;selection signals from sampling variance', 'Multi-level&#10;genetic architecture signatures: conserved sequence evolution&#10;versus rapidly changing gene-family composition']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate paper’s lifecycle-&#10;explicit selection framework into a sex-aware, environmentally&#10;contingent model that partitions reproductive fitness effects&#10;across timing, mate acquisition, gametic/post-insemination&#10;interactions, and survival trade-offs. Use sex-specific...&#10;justification: The candidate paper provides a structural&#10;abstraction directly relevant to the original problem: it&#10;connects selection to explicit lifecycle stages, distinguishes&#10;sex-specific effects, and quantifies conflict and genetic&#10;load. These mechanisms can be generalized into the reque...&#10;abstract: Sexual reproduction is a fundamental process that&#10;structures populations and modulates interactions between&#10;species. The reproductive process is shaped by selection&#10;acting on the variance in mating success. Additionally,&#10;conflict between the sexes over the mechanisms by which...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Sexual selection studies: A NESCent catalyst meeting&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Frequency-dependent sexual selection&#10;versus density-dependent fecundity selection as an organizing&#10;decomposition', 'Heritability/evolvability conditions for&#10;detecting and sustaining sexual selection', 'Explicit&#10;separation of sexual selection, natural selection, and&#10;fecundity selection as coupled but distinct fitness&#10;components', 'Conflict-theoretic framing for sex-specific&#10;interests and constraints']&#10;combination_plan: Use the frequency-dependent versus density-&#10;dependent distinction to structure a sex-aware fitness model&#10;in which mate acquisition and sexual selection are frequency-&#10;dependent processes while reproductive output and survival&#10;trade-offs are density- or environment-dependent proc...&#10;justification: Although the candidate is a conceptual&#10;synthesis rather than a formal method, it provides a&#10;transferable organizing abstraction: separating reproductive&#10;fitness drivers by their dynamical dependence structure and by&#10;selection type. This maps directly onto the original&#10;problem'...&#10;abstract: A catalyst meeting on sexual selection studies was&#10;held in July 2013 at the facilities of the National&#10;Evolutionary Synthesis Center (NESCent) in Durham, NC. This&#10;article by a subcommittee of the participants foregrounds some&#10;of the topics discussed at the meeting. Topics ment...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: economics&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: An optimisation principle for sex allocation in a&#10;temporally varying environment&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Optimization principle for sex&#10;allocation', 'Temporally varying environment as a contingency&#10;structure', 'Trade-off analysis between alternative&#10;reproductive investments', 'Fitness-maximizing allocation&#10;rules under environmental fluctuation']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate’s optimization framework&#10;to model sex-specific reproductive strategies as dynamic&#10;allocation policies whose fitness and evolvability depend on&#10;environmental state, genetic architecture, and mating ecology.&#10;Use its temporally contingent allocation logic to i...&#10;justification: The candidate paper addresses sex allocation&#10;under temporal environmental variation, which maps directly&#10;onto the original problem’s need for a sex-aware,&#10;environmentally contingent framework. Its optimization and&#10;trade-off structure can be abstracted to jointly model&#10;genetic,...&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: economics&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Resource Elasticity of Offspring Survival and the&#10;Optimal Evolution of Sex Ratios&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Resource-budget allocation trade-offs&#10;between adult survival and offspring survival', 'Elasticity or&#10;sensitivity of offspring survival to parental resource&#10;investment', 'Sex-specific optimal allocation under&#10;environmentally contingent selection', 'Hierarchical&#10;allocation rules where maintenance/survival is prioritized&#10;before reproductive investment']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate’s elasticity-based&#10;resource allocation framework into a sex-aware life-history&#10;model in which genetic architecture, mating ecology, and&#10;environmental conditions determine resource acquisition and&#10;allocation to survival, mate acquisition, and reproduction.&#10;U...&#10;justification: The candidate paper offers a transferable&#10;abstraction: environmentally contingent optimization of&#10;limited resources across survival and offspring fitness&#10;components with sex-ratio or sex-specific consequences. This&#10;maps directly onto the problem’s need to integrate&#10;reproductiv...&#10;abstract: The fitness of any organisms includes the survival&#10;and reproductive rate of adults and the survival of their&#10;offspring. Environmental selection pressures might not affect&#10;these two aspects of an organism equally. Assuming that an&#10;organism first allocates its limited resources...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 5&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Agent-Based Modelling as a Tool to Advance Evolutionary&#10;Population Theory&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Agent-based modelling as an integrative&#10;framework for evolutionary population dynamics', 'Explicit&#10;representation of heterogeneous individuals with sex,&#10;genotype, and life-history state', 'Local interaction rules&#10;for mate acquisition, sexual selection, and reproduction-&#10;survival trade-offs', 'Environmentally contingent decision&#10;rules and feedbacks between ecology and evolution', 'Emergent&#10;population-level outcomes such as fitness, evolvability, and&#10;constraints from individual-level mechanisms']&#10;combination_plan: Use agent-based modelling to build a sex-&#10;aware, environmentally contingent evolutionary framework in&#10;which individuals carry genetic architectures for reproductive&#10;timing, mating success, and survival allocation, interact&#10;under defined mating ecologies, and produce emergent me...&#10;justification: The candidate paper’s core abstraction—agent-&#10;based modelling as a way to advance evolutionary population&#10;theory—maps directly onto the problem’s need to integrate&#10;multiple interacting drivers of reproduction across sexes and&#10;environments. Even without direct empirical overlap,...&#10;abstract:&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 6&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: A demogenetic individual based model for the evolution&#10;of traits and genome architecture under sexual selection&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Demogenetic individual-based modeling&#10;that couples demography, inheritance, trait evolution, and&#10;mating dynamics', 'Explicit representation of genetic&#10;architecture, including genetic correlations and genome&#10;structure', 'Feedback between sexual selection and the&#10;evolution of mating traits and competitiveness', 'Sex-specific&#10;selection and trait expression within a population-dynamic&#10;framework']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate’s demogenetic&#10;individual-based modeling structure as a scaffold for a sex-&#10;aware, environmentally contingent framework in which genetic&#10;architecture, mate acquisition, sexual selection, and&#10;reproduction-survival trade-offs are represented as&#10;interacting indi...&#10;justification: The candidate paper provides a mechanistic&#10;abstraction for jointly modeling genetic architecture, sexual&#10;selection, and demographic dynamics, which maps directly onto&#10;the problem’s need to integrate isolated reproductive drivers&#10;into a predictive, sex-aware framework.&#10;abstract: Abstract Sexual selection has long been known to&#10;favor the evolution of mating behaviors such as mate&#10;preference and competitiveness, and to affect their genetic&#10;architecture, for instance by favoring genetic correlation&#10;between some traits. Reciprocally, genetic architecture..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#f9cb9c;strokeColor=#b45f06;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="7496" width="620" height="3372" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step7_analogy_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step7" target="step7_analogy_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step8" value="Step 8 Inspiration Selection&#10;status: completed&#10;selected_count: 3&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="10968" width="232" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step7_step_step8" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step7" target="step_step8"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step8_selected_panel" value="Step 8 Selected Inspiration&#10;count: 3&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 6&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: A demogenetic individual based model for the evolution&#10;of traits and genome architecture under sexual selection&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Demogenetic individual-based modeling&#10;that couples demography, inheritance, trait evolution, and&#10;mating dynamics', 'Explicit representation of genetic&#10;architecture, including genetic correlations and genome&#10;structure', 'Feedback between sexual selection and the&#10;evolution of mating traits and competitiveness', 'Sex-specific&#10;selection and trait expression within a population-dynamic&#10;framework']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate’s demogenetic&#10;individual-based modeling structure as a scaffold for a sex-&#10;aware, environmentally contingent framework in which genetic&#10;architecture, mate acquisition, sexual selection, and&#10;reproduction-survival trade-offs are represented as&#10;interacting indi...&#10;justification: The candidate paper provides a mechanistic&#10;abstraction for jointly modeling genetic architecture, sexual&#10;selection, and demographic dynamics, which maps directly onto&#10;the problem’s need to integrate isolated reproductive drivers&#10;into a predictive, sex-aware framework.&#10;abstract: Abstract Sexual selection has long been known to&#10;favor the evolution of mating behaviors such as mate&#10;preference and competitiveness, and to affect their genetic&#10;architecture, for instance by favoring genetic correlation&#10;between some traits. Reciprocally, genetic architecture...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: economics&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Resource Elasticity of Offspring Survival and the&#10;Optimal Evolution of Sex Ratios&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Resource-budget allocation trade-offs&#10;between adult survival and offspring survival', 'Elasticity or&#10;sensitivity of offspring survival to parental resource&#10;investment', 'Sex-specific optimal allocation under&#10;environmentally contingent selection', 'Hierarchical&#10;allocation rules where maintenance/survival is prioritized&#10;before reproductive investment']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate’s elasticity-based&#10;resource allocation framework into a sex-aware life-history&#10;model in which genetic architecture, mating ecology, and&#10;environmental conditions determine resource acquisition and&#10;allocation to survival, mate acquisition, and reproduction.&#10;U...&#10;justification: The candidate paper offers a transferable&#10;abstraction: environmentally contingent optimization of&#10;limited resources across survival and offspring fitness&#10;components with sex-ratio or sex-specific consequences. This&#10;maps directly onto the problem’s need to integrate&#10;reproductiv...&#10;abstract: The fitness of any organisms includes the survival&#10;and reproductive rate of adults and the survival of their&#10;offspring. Environmental selection pressures might not affect&#10;these two aspects of an organism equally. Assuming that an&#10;organism first allocates its limited resources...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: economics&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: An optimisation principle for sex allocation in a&#10;temporally varying environment&#10;plausible: True&#10;combination_points: ['Optimization principle for sex&#10;allocation', 'Temporally varying environment as a contingency&#10;structure', 'Trade-off analysis between alternative&#10;reproductive investments', 'Fitness-maximizing allocation&#10;rules under environmental fluctuation']&#10;combination_plan: Adapt the candidate’s optimization framework&#10;to model sex-specific reproductive strategies as dynamic&#10;allocation policies whose fitness and evolvability depend on&#10;environmental state, genetic architecture, and mating ecology.&#10;Use its temporally contingent allocation logic to i...&#10;justification: The candidate paper addresses sex allocation&#10;under temporal environmental variation, which maps directly&#10;onto the original problem’s need for a sex-aware,&#10;environmentally contingent framework. Its optimization and&#10;trade-off structure can be abstracted to jointly model&#10;genetic,...&#10;abstract:" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#ead1dc;strokeColor=#a64d79;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="10968" width="620" height="1680" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step8_selected_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step8" target="step8_selected_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step9" value="Step 9 Idea Generation&#10;status: completed&#10;idea_count: 1&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="12748" width="220" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step8_step_step9" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step8" target="step_step9"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step9_ideas_panel" value="Step 9 Idea Generation + Novelty Check&#10;count: 1&#10;&#10;1. Deferred reproductive capital: sex-specific invasion&#10;thresholds and sexually antagonistic cohort storage as drivers&#10;of reproductive evolvability&#10;novelty_level: medium&#10;motivation: The original query asks what drives reproduction in&#10;living systems. In evolutionary ecology, a key answer is that&#10;heritable variation in reproductive timing shapes evolvability&#10;[Author et al., 2025](https://openalex.org/W4416670776),&#10;nonrandom mating can alter genetic variance and adaptation under&#10;changing climates [Author et al.,&#10;2021](https://openalex.org/W3141331266), sex-specific selection&#10;assigns different fi...&#10;methods: 1. Deferred reproductive capital state and sex-specific&#10;conversion functions: Represent each individual as having sex-&#10;specific deferred reproductive capital K_s, refractory delay&#10;tau_s, decay rate delta_s, maintenance cost m_s, and survival&#10;cost lambda_s. At each reproductive bout, an individual divides&#10;available reproductive resources between immediate effort and&#10;K_s. Female K_s converts into future gamete or offspring&#10;production, while male K_s converts into future mate attraction,&#10;competition, or mating effor...&#10;novelty_justification: Relative to the provided references, the&#10;closest existing combination is the set of all four: genetic&#10;variance in reproductive timing as a source of evolvability,&#10;sex-specific selection on reproductive traits, assortative&#10;mating and polygenic adaptation under environmental change, and&#10;asymmetric reproduction-survival trade-offs shaping sex-biased&#10;longevity. Together these references motivate the ingredients of&#10;the...&#10;improvement_suggestions: ['Derive the dynamics of additive&#10;genetic variance explicitly and show that sexually antagonistic&#10;cohort storage maintains variance through a negative cross-sex,&#10;cross-cohort covariance generated specifically by deferred&#10;capital, making the mechanism mathematically distinct from&#10;ordinary antagonistic pleiotropy or generic balancing&#10;selection.', 'Make evolvability endogenous to deferred capital&#10;by deriving a feedback in which stored capital changes the&#10;expression or selection gradient of reproductive-timing alleles,&#10;so deferred capital can invade because it alters future response&#10;to selection rather than only because it improves individual&#10;fitness under unreliable cues.']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#9673a6;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="12748" width="680" height="924" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step9_ideas_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step9" target="step9_ideas_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="retrieval_1" value="Retrieval 1&#10;step: step1&#10;command: search-papers&#10;cache_hit: False&#10;paper_count: 6&#10;&#10;Parameters&#10;query_text: sperm competition postcopulatory sexual&#10;selection fertilization success&#10;keywords: [{'text': 'sperm competition', 'score': 10},&#10;{'text': 'competition postcopulatory', 'score': 9},&#10;{'text': 'postcopulatory sexual', 'score': 8}, {'text':&#10;'sexual selection', 'score': 7}, {'text': 'selection&#10;fertilization', 'score': 6}]&#10;source_type: idea_text&#10;top_k: 18&#10;retrieval_mode: hybrid&#10;target_field: Chemistry&#10;&#10;Returned Papers&#10;1. Sperm competition in three species of ground-dwelling&#10;squirrels: intraspecific and interspecific differences&#10;(2020) [score=0.5011]&#10;Abstract: Sperm competition is a post-copulatory&#10;evolutionary process that can affect sperm production, and&#10;consequently reproductive success. Successful males may&#10;not necessarily be the males with the highest number of&#10;sperm. Other traits such as sperm motility or abnormality&#10;may also...&#10;&#10;2. The ’omics revolution and our understanding of sperm&#10;cell biology (2010) [score=0.4279]&#10;Abstract: The foundations of proteomics are to study gene&#10;products and their regulatory roles within cells.&#10;Paradoxically, the only evidence that sperm cells make new&#10;proteins is through mitochondrial protein synthesis. Yet&#10;despite this, spermatozoa are the perfect candidates for&#10;mass s...&#10;&#10;3. Semiochemical techniques for the management of coconut&#10;insect pest (2024) [score=0.3614]&#10;Abstract: Semiochemicals are chemical substances utilized&#10;by insects use to modify their physiology and behaviour&#10;and are classified as pheromones (intraspecific effects)&#10;and allelochemicals (interspecific effects). It plays a&#10;crucial role in Integrated Pest Management (IPM) as a&#10;marker...&#10;&#10;4. Human cyclic nucleotide phosphodiesterases possess a&#10;much broader substrate-specificity than previously&#10;appreciated (2011) [score=0.3595]&#10;Abstract: Phosphodiesterases (PDEs) capable of degrading&#10;cAMP and cGMP are indispensable for the regulation of&#10;cyclic nucleotide-mediated signals. The existence of other&#10;cyclic nucleotides such as cCMP and cUMP has been&#10;discussed controversially in the literature. Despite&#10;publications o...&#10;&#10;5. Energy-dispersive EXAFS to study chemical reactions:&#10;the case of electron transfer reaction of hydroquinone&#10;(2001) [score=0.346]&#10;Abstract: N/A&#10;&#10;6. Optimized sperm selection (2024) [score=0.2015]&#10;Abstract: N/A" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="2400" y="340" width="442" height="1248" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_event_1" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step1" target="retrieval_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="retrieval_2" value="Retrieval 2&#10;step: step1&#10;command: search-papers&#10;cache_hit: False&#10;paper_count: 10&#10;&#10;Parameters&#10;query_text: quantitative genetic heritability reproductive&#10;traits adaptation&#10;keywords: [{'text': 'quantitative genetic', 'score': 10},&#10;{'text': 'genetic heritability', 'score': 9}, {'text':&#10;'heritability reproductive', 'score': 8}, {'text':&#10;'reproductive traits', 'score': 7}, {'text': 'traits&#10;adaptation', 'score': 6}]&#10;source_type: idea_text&#10;top_k: 18&#10;retrieval_mode: hybrid&#10;target_field